    {
      "unit_id": "qrm-control-chart-l9-note-f6d674a13145:p0003:text",
      "course_id": "qrm",
      "document_id": "qrm-control-chart-l9-note-f6d674a13145",
      "kind": "page_text",
      "text": "Benefits of Control Charts\n• When to take corrective action.\n• Type of remedial action necessary.\n• When to leave a process alone.\n• Process capability.\n• Possible means of quality improvement.\n\nCause of Variations:\n• Special Cause (As per Deming 15%): If an observation falls outside the\n  control limits or a non-random pattern is exhibited, special causes are\n assumed to exist, and the process is said to be out of control.\n• Common Cause (As per Deming 85%): If quality characteristic values\n  are within control limits and no non-random pattern is visible, it is\n assumed that a system of common causes exists and that the process\n  is in a state of statistical control.\n\n\n\n                                        ME3327E QUALITY, RELIABILITY AND\n                                               MAINTENANCE",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "slide": 3,
        "bbox": null
      },
